<h2>Rules</h2>
The 2023-2024 competition had a theme of urban mobility where "pilots" (crew) flew passengers and were used for medical transport. The overall rules included these constraints on the aircraft:

~ Maximum of 5 ft wingspan

~ In parking configuration, the aircraft must fit in a 2.5 ft wide space

~ Passengers are wooden dolls of size 3.5 in tall and a base diameter of 1.5 in (exact dolls provided)

~ Pilots are separate and in front of the passengers and/or EMTs and the patient.


Missions

Ground Mission: Time for to put plane in flight ready configuration, load passengers, unload passengers, put plane in parking configuration

Mission 1: Crew only flight, 3 laps

Mission 2: Crew + EMTs + patient+ Medical Supply Cabinet, 3 laps

Mission 3: Crew and Passengers, 5 minute window


Goals

~ Maximize medical supply cabinet weight 

~ Maximize number of passengers

~ Minimize lap times

~ Minimize ground mission time by making efficient loading and parking systems



<h2>Swivel Wing</h2>
To meet the competition's parking constraint (2.50 ft width), Ursa Major required a wing that could rotate 90 degrees to align with the fuselage. I was responsible for the design and fabrication of the swivel wing integration piece, the critical mechanical interface between the wing spar and fuselage.

The integration piece consisted of two 3D-printed Onyx components joined around a ball bearing, allowing relative rotation while maintaining structural integrity under aerodynamic loads. I worked with a fellow team member to include the following constraints:

~ clearances to achieve sufficient friction for ground handling

~ locking features to prevent in-flight rotation

~ ensure compatibility with the carbon fiber spars




<h2>Wing Manufacturing</h2>
I contributed directly to the manufacturing and assembly of the wing, a tapered high-wing with flaperon control surfaces. My work included:


~ Laser-cutting ribs, stringers, and servo plates from basswood and balsa

~ Assembling the wing structure using wood glue and epoxy

~ Installing the experimental dowel rod hinge system for control surfaces, including polystyrene foam leading edges to reduce aerodynamic gaps


I also took responsibility for servo validation, ensuring the EcoPower 640T servos provided sufficient torque (64 oz-in) to deflect control surfaces against aerodynamic loads—directly addressing a failure mode identified in Iteration 1 flight testing.
